    <article class="post-content reveal">

        <p>Every legitimate cannabis or research chemical product should come with a <strong>Certificate of Analysis (COA)</strong> — a document from a third-party laboratory verifying what is actually in the product. But here is the problem: most people have no idea how to read one. A COA is not just a piece of paper with a percentage on it. It is a technical report with chromatograms, tables, abbreviations, and pass/fail criteria that require interpretation.</p>

        <p>This guide walks you through a real COA section by section. By the end, you will know how to verify potency, spot contaminants, read a chromatogram, and identify fake lab reports from a mile away.</p>

        <h2>Anatomy of a COA: The Visual Breakdown</h2>

        <p>Below is a simplified mockup of what a typical COA looks like. Each labeled section corresponds to a critical piece of information you should verify before trusting any product.</p>

        <!-- VISUAL: COA MOCKUP -->
        <div class="coa-mockup reveal">
            <div class="coa-header-line">
                <div class="coa-logo-placeholder">LAB</div>
