        <h2>Decoding the Abbreviations</h2>

        <p>COAs are full of shorthand that can confuse even experienced buyers. Here is a quick-reference table of the most common abbreviations you will encounter.</p>

        <!-- VISUAL: ABBREVIATIONS TABLE -->
        <div class="abbr-table-wrap reveal">
            <table class="abbr-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Abbreviation</th><th>Full Term</th><th>What It Means</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">ND</span></td><td>Not Detected</td><td>The compound was tested for but found below the detection limit</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">NT</span></td><td>Not Tested</td><td>This panel was not analyzed for this sample</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">LOQ</span></td><td>Limit of Quantitation</td><td>The lowest amount that can be reliably measured</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">LOD</span></td><td>Limit of Detection</td><td>The lowest amount that can be detected (but not precisely quantified)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">ppm</span></td><td>Parts Per Million</td><td>Microgram of contaminant per gram of product (1 ppm = 1 μg/g)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">ppb</span></td><td>Parts Per Billion</td><td>Nanogram per gram — used for ultra-trace contaminants like mycotoxins</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">CFU/g</span></td><td>Colony Forming Units</td><td>Measure of microbial contamination per gram</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">HPLC</span></td><td>High-Performance Liquid Chromatography</td><td>Method used for potency and pesticide testing</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">GC-MS</span></td><td>Gas Chromatography-Mass Spectrometry</td><td>Method used for residual solvents and terpenes</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">ICP-MS</span></td><td>Inductively Coupled Plasma MS</td><td>Method used for heavy metal detection</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">qPCR</span></td><td>Quantitative PCR</td><td>Method used for microbial DNA detection</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="abbr-code">COA</span></td><td>Certificate of Analysis</td><td>The document you are reading right now</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h2>Understanding the Six Test Panels</h2>

        <p>A comprehensive COA covers six distinct testing areas. Each uses different instrumentation and has different pass/fail thresholds. Here is what each panel actually tells you.</p>

        <!-- VISUAL: PANEL CARDS -->
        <div class="panel-grid reveal">
            <div class="panel-card">
                <div class="panel-icon potency">📊</div>
                <div class="panel-name">Potency</div>
                <div class="panel-desc">HPLC quantifies THC, CBD, and minor cannabinoids. Look for total cannabinoids above 95% in distillate.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="panel-card">
                <div class="panel-icon pesticide">🌿</div>
                <div class="panel-name">Pesticides</div>
                <div class="panel-desc">LC-MS/MS screens 60+ chemicals. All must be ND or below action limits. No exceptions.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="panel-card">
                <div class="panel-icon microbial">🦠</div>
                <div class="panel-name">Microbials</div>
                <div class="panel-desc">Tests for E. coli, Salmonella, Aspergillus, and total mold. Distillate should pass easily.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="panel-card">
                <div class="panel-icon heavy">⚗️</div>
                <div class="panel-name">Heavy Metals</div>
                <div class="panel-desc">ICP-MS detects arsenic, cadmium, lead, mercury. Cannabis bioaccumulates metals from soil.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="panel-card">
                <div class="panel-icon solvent">💨</div>
                <div class="panel-name">Residual Solvents</div>
                <div class="panel-desc">GC-MS finds leftover butane, ethanol, propane. Limits set by FDA Q3C guidance.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="panel-card">
                <div class="panel-icon mycotoxin">☠️</div>
                <div class="panel-name">Mycotoxins</div>
                <div class="panel-desc">LC-MS/MS screens for aflatoxins and ochratoxins — toxic mold byproducts measured in ppb.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h2>How to Spot a Fake COA</h2>

        <p>Fake COAs are more common than you think. Unscrupulous vendors copy real reports, change product names, or forge lab logos entirely. Here is how to tell the difference.</p>

        <!-- VISUAL: FAKE SPOTTER -->
        <div class="fake-spotter-grid reveal">
            <div class="spotter-card">
                <div class="spotter-icon spotter-bad">✕</div>
                <div class="spotter-text"><strong>No Lab License Number</strong>Every legitimate testing lab has a state-issued license. If the COA does not list one, it is not from a real lab.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="spotter-card">
                <div class="spotter-icon spotter-good">✓</div>
                <div class="spotter-text"><strong>Verify the Lab</strong>Call the lab directly using the phone number on their official website — not the COA. Ask them to confirm the sample ID and batch number.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="spotter-card">
                <div class="spotter-icon spotter-bad">✕</div>
                <div class="spotter-text"><strong>Generic or Missing Chromatogram</strong>A real COA includes chromatograms or spectra. A single number with no supporting data is meaningless.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="spotter-card">
                <div class="spotter-icon spotter-good">✓</div>
                <div class="spotter-text"><strong>Cross-Check Dates</strong>The sample date, test date, and report date should make logical sense. A sample collected yesterday cannot have a month-long microbial test completed today.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="spotter-card">
                <div class="spotter-icon spotter-bad">✕</div>
                <div class="spotter-text"><strong>Pixelated or Copied Signatures</strong>Real COAs have actual signatures or digital certificates. A blurry stamp or obviously pasted signature is a forgery red flag.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="spotter-card">
                <div class="spotter-icon spotter-good">✓</div>
                <div class="spotter-text"><strong>Match the Batch Number</strong>The batch number on the COA must match the batch number on your product packaging. Mismatches mean the COA was copied from another product.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h2>Why COAs Matter for Concentrates</h2>

        <p>Here is a fact most consumers do not realize: <strong>concentration magnifies contamination</strong>. A cannabis flower with 100 ppm of a pesticide might be below legal limits. But when that flower is processed into distillate, the same 100 ppm becomes 1,000 ppm because everything except cannabinoids is removed. The contaminant stays. The plant matter goes.</p>

        <p>This is why distillate and concentrate COAs are stricter than flower COAs. And it is why a vendor selling "95% pure" distillate without a full panel COA is asking you to trust them with your health. Do not.</p>

        <p>The <a href="https://www.aoac.org/scientific-solutions/standards/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AOAC International</a> sets the gold-standard analytical methods for cannabis testing, and their standards are adopted by most state regulatory frameworks. When a lab says they follow "AOAC methods," it means their procedures have been validated against the most rigorous protocols in the industry.</p>

        <h2>Reading Between the Lines</h2>

        <p>Sometimes what a COA does <em>not</em> say is as important as what it does. Here are subtle tricks vendors use:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Selective testing:</strong> A COA that shows potency but skips pesticide or solvent panels. They are hiding something.</li>
            <li><strong>Old dates:</strong> A COA from six months ago for a "fresh batch." Testing degrades over time, especially for volatile compounds.</li>
            <li><strong>Vague product names:</strong> "Cannabis extract" instead of "Delta-9 THC Distillate (Batch D9-2026-0847)." Specificity matters.</li>
            <li><strong>Rounding up:</strong> A potency of 89.4% rounded to "90%+" in marketing. The COA shows the truth; the label shows the lie.</li>
            <li><strong>Missing minor cannabinoids:</strong> If a distillate claims 98% THC but the COA only shows 85% total cannabinoids, the remaining 13% is mystery oil.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

        <p>A Certificate of Analysis is not optional paperwork. It is the only objective proof of what you are actually buying. Learning to read one empowers you to cut through marketing claims and hold vendors accountable.</p>

        <p>At 247AVL Plug, we provide full-panel COAs for every batch of THC distillate, every vial of ketamine, and every research chemical we stock. We list batch numbers. We name the testing lab. We include chromatograms. And we encourage you to verify — because transparency is not a feature, it is the minimum standard.</p>

        <p>If a vendor cannot or will not provide a COA, the question is not whether their product is clean. The question is what they are hiding.</p>

